{
 "slug": "js_syntax",
 "course": "js",
 "order": 4,
 "url": "/js/js_syntax.asp",
 "title": "JavaScript Syntax",
 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "h1",
   "text": "JavaScript Syntax"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "Syntax Rules"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "Syntax are the rules how programs must be constructed:"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "// How to Declare variables:\nlet x = 5;\nlet y = 6;\n\n// How to Compute values:\nlet z = x + y;\n\n// I am a Comment. I do Nothing"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "JavaScript Values"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "The JavaScript syntax defines two types of values:"
  },
  {
   "type": "list",
   "ordered": false,
   "items": [
    "Literals (Fixed values)",
    "Variables (Variable values)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "JavaScript Literals"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "The most important syntax rules for literals (fixed values) are:"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Numbers are written with or without decimals:"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "10.50\n\n1001",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_syntax_numbers"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Strings are text, written within double or single quotes:"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "\"John Doe\"\n\n'John Doe'",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_syntax_strings"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "JavaScript Keywords"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "JavaScript keywords are used to defines actions to be performed."
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "The\nlet and const keywords create variables:"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = 5;\n\nconst fname = \"John\";",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_syntax_keywords"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "JavaScript keywords are case-sensitive."
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "JavaScript does not interpret LET or Let as the keyword let."
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "JavaScript Variables"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Variables are containers for storing data values."
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Variables must be identified with unique names."
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "// Define x as a variable\n\nlet x;\n\n// Assign the value 6 to x\n\nx = 6;",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_syntax_variables"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "JavaScript Identifiers"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "An identifier is the name you give to a variable."
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Rules for identifiers:"
  },
  {
   "type": "list",
   "ordered": false,
   "items": [
    "Must start with a letter, _, or $",
    "Can contain digits after the first character",
    "Cannot be a reserved keyword (let, const, if, etc.)",
    "Are case-sensitive"
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "JavaScript Operators"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "JavaScript assignment operators (=) assign values to variables:"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = 5;\n\nlet y = 6;\n\nlet sum = x + y;",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_syntax_assign"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "JavaScript uses arithmetic operators ( + - * / ) to\ncompute values:"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "5 * 10",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_syntax_operators"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "JavaScript Expressions"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "An expression is a combination of values, variables, and operators,\nwhich computes to a value."
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "(5 + 6) * 10",
   "caption": "Examples"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "x * 10",
   "caption": "Examples"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "\"John\" + \" \" + \"Doe\"",
   "caption": "Examples"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_syntax_expressions"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "JavaScript is Case Sensitive"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "JavaScript identifiers are case sensitive."
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "The variables lastName and lastname,\nare different variables:"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let lastName = \"Doe\";\n\nlet lastname = \"Peterson\";",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_syntax_case"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "JavaScript and Camel Case"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Historically, programmers have used different ways of joining multiple words into one variable name:"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Hyphens:"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "first-name, last-name, master-card, inter-city."
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Hyphens are not allowed in JavaScript. They are reserved for subtractions."
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Underscore:"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "first_name, last_name, master_card, inter_city."
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Upper Camel Case (Pascal Case):"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "FirstName, LastName, MasterCard, InterCity."
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Lower Camel Case:"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "firstName, lastName, masterCard, interCity."
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "JavaScript programmers tend to use lower camel case."
  }
 ]
}